# Verifying a push, and reading a push failure

Reporting "pushed" is the one claim in an audit that the user acts on without
re-checking, so it is the one claim most worth proving. Two failure modes
account for nearly all of it: a stale cache reported as proof, and a failure
whose message names the wrong cause.

## A remote-tracking ref is a cache, not proof

`git log @{u}..HEAD`, `git rev-parse origin/main`, and `git status` all read
the **local** remote-tracking ref, last updated by the last fetch or push. None
of them contact the server. After a push attempt fails, every one of them
keeps reporting the pre-push state — including the cheerful `0 unpushed`.

```sh
git log --oneline @{u}..HEAD | wc -l    # -> 0   NOT proof of anything
```

In one session this produced a confident "pushed and verified", read off a
cached ref, for a push that had in fact not been confirmed. The correction is
one line, but the user had already been told the work was delivered.

**Always ask the server, never the cache:**

```sh
git ls-remote origin                       # ref actually on the remote
git fetch origin && git log --oneline @{u}..HEAD | wc -l
```

`git ls-remote` is the only one of these that authenticates. If it cannot
reach the remote, you have no evidence either way — say "unconfirmed", not
"pushed" and not "failed".

After a successful push, confirm the fix is *on the remote*, not just locally:

```sh
git show origin/master:<file> | grep -c '<the marker you just added>'
```

## Push failures name the wrong cause; match on the message

The error text points at a symptom, so the remedy is not always what the text
suggests. Distinguish these four before acting:

| Message | Actual cause | What it is not |
|---|---|---|
| `src refspec main does not match any` | the branch is not named `main` | a remote problem |
| `Repository not found` | the remote repo does not exist / is not visible to this key | a bad path or a failed auth |
| `Permission denied (publickey)` | the server rejected the key | anything local |
| `could not read Username for 'https://…'` | no credential for the HTTPS transport | a broken remote |

`src refspec … does not match any` is purely local and is fixed by reading the
branch:

```sh
git branch --show-current
git branch -r | head            # what the remote actually has
git remote -v                   # and which URL, since it may be ssh vs https
```

Never assume a branch name. Repositories that coexisted with peers, or that
were created by a tool, are on `master` while newer ones default to `main`.

`Repository not found` is the ambiguous one: it reads like an auth failure but
is usually just "this repository has never been created." git-over-SSH
**cannot create a repository** — only push to an existing one — so no amount of
retrying, and no second agent on the same box, will clear it. The user has to
create an empty repo (or supply a correctly-scoped token). Say that in one
sentence, with the exact command to run afterwards, and prepare everything
else so the push is a single step when they do.

## When a credential that worked minutes ago stops working

Auth that succeeds and then fails mid-session is remote-side revocation, not
something you changed. Do not re-verify local setup you already verified —
diff the evidence:

```sh
ssh -o BatchMode=yes -T git@github.com          # the error
timeout 10 bash -c 'cat </dev/null >/dev/tcp/github.com/22' && echo reachable
ssh -v -o BatchMode=yes -T git@github.com 2>&1 | grep -E 'identity file|Authentications'
```

`identity file … type 2` means the key was **found and offered** — so the
server saw it and refused it. That is a remote-side answer, and no local
reconfiguration will change it. Reassuring the user that their setup is fine is
only correct *after* this check; before it, it is a guess.

Hand the user something they can act on directly: the key fingerprint to
compare against their account's key list.

```sh
ssh-keygen -lf ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
# 256 SHA256:…  user@host (ED25519)
```

Do not retry a rejected credential repeatedly — see the lockout rule in
SKILL.md. One confirmation, then the fingerprint, then stop.

## Check for a remote before promising anything about one

A commit request is not a push request, and a repo may have no remote at all.
`git remote -v` printing nothing is a legitimate state, not a broken one — do
not add a remote on your own initiative just so a push becomes possible. That
publishes work the user may not have intended to publish, and for a hardening
toolset the contents describe the machine's exact posture.

Survey all repos under audit in one pass, before writing any commit message
that implies delivery:

```sh
for d in ~/Projects/*/; do
  [ -d "$d/.git" ] || continue
  printf '%-26s %s\n' "$(basename "$d")" \
    "$(git -C "$d" remote -v | head -1 || echo 'NO REMOTE (local only)')"
done
```

`git rev-parse --abbrev-ref '@{u}'` failing with *no upstream configured* is
the same fact from the other side: local-only, nothing to push, nothing
unpushed. Report it as its own state rather than letting "committed" imply a
delivery step that does not exist. Offer to add a remote and push, and let the
user choose which repos — for a security toolchain that is a real decision, and
for a public repo it is a publication decision.

## Report the tri-state honestly

Per repo, one of three — and never collapse the middle one into the first:

- **pushed and confirmed** — `git ls-remote` shows the ref
- **committed locally, push unconfirmed** — auth broken, or remote absent
- **never created on the remote** — needs the user to create it

A fourth state exists and is not a failure: **no remote configured at all** —
a clean local commit with no delivery step in existence, nothing pending.

A user who believes an un-pushed change is delivered has been misled in the one
direction that matters most. When you walk back an earlier "verified" claim,
say so in one line and move on — no apology tour.
